IN THE COURT OF THE LEARNED CIVIL JUDGE / ADDITIONAL DISTRICT JUDGE, UDAIPUR

Civil Suit No. ______ of 2025

Smt. Kamla Devi                                                          … Plaintiff

Versus

Shri Vikram Singh                                                        … Defendant

WRITTEN ARGUMENTS ON BEHALF OF THE PLAINTIFF

I. The Plaintiff is the absolute owner and in continuous peaceful possession of House No. 22. The 10-ft passage on the eastern side has been used openly, peacefully and as of right for more than 20 years.

II. The Defendant has commenced digging and stacking material with the clear intention of blocking the passage. The photographs placed on record establish ongoing interference.

III. For temporary injunction the three classical tests are satisfied:
   (a) Prima facie case – title, possession and interference are proved.
   (b) Balance of convenience – the Plaintiff will lose access, light and air; the Defendant only suffers temporary restraint on construction.
   (c) Irreparable injury – loss of access cannot be adequately compensated in money.

IV. For permanent injunction the Plaintiff has established a legal right and actual or threatened invasion of that right. The Defendant has no superior right over the passage.

V. The defence of title to the strip and alleged delay are unsupported by reliable evidence and are liable to be rejected.

PRAYER
The suit may be decreed with costs and the temporary injunction already granted may be confirmed / made absolute.
